// ── STORY PLAN — written before the beats (references/story-structure.md) ──
const STORY = {
  "idea": "Children hide their inner world not because they distrust their parents but because they love them so much that disappointing them feels terrifying. Seven things they cannot say — listen, don't fix; I notice your phone; your stress becomes mine; stop comparing me; forgive me like you forgive yourself; I'm scared to disappoint you; love me most when I'm hardest to love — all point at one goal: a home where telling the truth feels safe.",
  "arc": "We open on a hug at the front door — and THE JAR SON hides behind his back, lid screwed tight. Not drugs, not secrets: thoughts, fears, tiny moments MOM forgot and he kept. Seven jars stand on his shelf; each chapter opens one. MOM learns to sit inside the blanket fort instead of launching an investigation; to look up from THE PHONE before the little boy with the frog stops talking and the teen behind the closed door stops trying; to say the one sentence that lifts the weight of his imagination; to stop measuring him against other marks on the door frame; to repair her own mistakes out loud (the gold-seamed mug); to make failure safe enough to bring home (the bad test pinned next to the good one) — and, in the storm of the slammed door and 'I hate you', to correct, wait and love all at once while his spikes soften.",
  "ending": "Cautious closeness — at the kitchen table SON unscrews THE JAR himself and tells MOM the truth while her phone lies face down; the front-door hug returns with nothing behind his back. Relief, not a perfect happy ending.",
};

